<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Francoist Spain</h1><p class="st-meta">1936 CE – 1975 CE · capital Madrid</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The dictatorship of Francisco Franco, born in the Spanish Civil War and lasting until his death in 1975.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the military coup of July 1936 against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a>, the rebel generals named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Francisco Franco</a> head of state and Generalísimo at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> on 1 October 1936. Backed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fascist-italy">Fascist Italy</a>, his Nationalists won the civil war in April 1939.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Franco</a> ruled through the army, the Church and a single party, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fet-y-de-las-jons">FET y de las JONS</a>, with harsh repression, executions and labour camps in the 1940s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> stayed out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, apart from sending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blue-division">Blue Division</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> alliance with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1953 ended isolation, and the Stabilisation Plan of 1959 brought rapid growth, tourism and urbanisation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1969 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Franco</a> named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-carlos-i">Juan Carlos de Borbón</a> as his successor. ETA killed Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-carrero-blanco">Luis Carrero Blanco</a> in 1973. Franco died on 20 November 1975, and Juan Carlos became king, opening the transition to the democratic <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-spain">Kingdom of Spain</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/francoist-spain.jpg" alt="Francoist Spain" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1945%E2%80%931977).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1936 CE – 1975 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Madrid</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Francisco Franco, Luis Carrero Blanco, Carlos Arias Navarro</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spanish State, Nationalist Spain, Estado Español</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=francoist-spain" aria-label="Francoist Spain on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4589 733 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Francoist Spain lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4909 989L4864 1148M4909 989L4854 1142M4909 989L4735 1360M4909 989L4655 1514M4909 989L4909 989M4909 989L5053 958M4909 989L4934 907M4909 989L4863 890M4909 989L4910 989M4909 989L4909 989"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4864 1148h0M4854 1142h0M4735 1360h0M4655 1514h0M4909 989h0M5053 958h0M4934 907h0M4863 890h0M4910 989h0M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4909 989h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4909 989h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Madrid. <a href="/atlas?year=1956&amp;focus=francoist-spain">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
